  "generated_text": "1. We start with the given equations:\n   \\[\n   x + y = 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   xy - z^2 = 1\n   \\]\n\n2. From the first equation, we can express \\( y \\) in terms of \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   y = 2 - x\n   \\]\n\n3. Substitute \\( y = 2 - x \\) into the second equation:\n   \\[\n   x(2 - x) - z^2 = 1\n   \\]\n\n4. Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   2x - x^2 - z^2 = 1\n   \\]\n\n5. Rearrange the equation to form a quadratic in \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   -x^2 + 2x - z^2 - 1 = 0\n   \\]\n\n6. This is a quadratic equation in \\( x \\). However, since the discriminant of this quadratic equation is negative, there are no real solutions for \\( x \\) in the real numbers. Therefore, there are no real solutions.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[0, '0']",
    "gold_answer": "Squaring the equation $x + y = 2,$ we get $x^2 + 2xy + y^2 = 4.$  Also, $4xy - 4z^2 = 4,$ so\n\\[x^2 + 2xy + y^2 = 4xy - 4z^2.\\]Then $x^2 - 2xy + y^2 + 4z^2 = 0,$ which we write as\n\\[(x - y)^2 + 4z^2 = 0.\\]For this equation to hold, we must have $x = y$ and $z = 0,$ and if $x = y,$ then $x = y = 1.$\n\nTherefore, there is only $\\boxed{1}$ solution, namely $(x,y,z) = (1,1,0).$",
    "is_correct": false
  },
